ISO 226:2023, Table 1
---------------------

The per-frequency coefficients of Table 1 (`alpha_f`, `L_U` and `T_f`, 29
values each) of

    ISO 226:2023, Acoustics -- Normal equal-loudness-level contours
    (c) International Organization for Standardization

are **not reproduced in this repository.** They are loaded at runtime from
`reference/iso226_table1.py`, which is gitignored and which each user supplies
from their own copy of the standard. The template and instructions are in
`tests/iso226_table1.py.example`.

This is not a token gesture: ISO 226 Formula (1) cannot be evaluated without
those coefficients, so without that file the software has no function. Nothing
here reconstructs, approximates or works around them.

Permission to reproduce Table 1 was requested and, pending an answer, the
coefficients have been removed rather than shipped on an assumption. ISO
customer service confirmed on 1 August 2026 that reproducing Table 1
constitutes reproduction of ISO content, that it requires explicit permission
or a licensing agreement rather than being covered automatically by fair use,
and that such permissions are handled by the national ISO member body -- ANSI
in the United States. A request is with ANSI. **If permission is granted, the
coefficients may be restored and this notice updated to say so.**

The generated presets in `REW/` are this project's own fitted filter values,
not ISO's, and remain usable without the standard.

Nothing else from the standard is reproduced here beyond short quotations of
the Scope and of the stated range of validity of Formula (1). Annex B is
excluded on the same basis: it is used only to verify this implementation, the
values are not in this repository, and the test that uses them is disabled
unless a user supplies them from their own copy of the standard.

**This project is not a substitute for the standard.** Anyone doing serious
work with equal-loudness contours should buy it:
https://www.iso.org/standard/83117.html


ISO 266
-------

`ISO_FREQ` in `iso226_utils.py` is the preferred one-third-octave frequency
series of ISO 266. It is retained because the Table 1 columns must be indexed
against something, and because the series is the R10 preferred-number series --
20, 25, 31.5, 40 and so on -- which is a rounded geometric progression rather
than measured data.
